-</section><section class="section-block final-assay" data-reveal="" id="kinetic-pilot"><span class="anchor-alias" id="odor-screen-20261021"></span><p class="final-assay-kicker">05 · Odor testing</p><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Odor testing</h2><p>We followed fluorescence over time in ten OR + ORCO conditions, ORCO-only cells and untransfected cells. Figures 11–14 show Test 1 and Test 2 time courses, condition comparisons, control-referenced maps and the Test 2 VUAA1-referenced map.</p><p>Test 2 contains 72 wells and 1,440 readings across ten OR + ORCO conditions, ORCO-only cells and untransfected cells. Each construct × stimulus has one well.</p><p>VUAA1 is an established Orco agonist used in heterologous receptor assays. <a aria-label="Reference 2" class="cite" href="#p2-ref-jones" id="cite-p2-jones">[2]</a> <a aria-label="Reference 3" class="cite" href="#p2-ref-zboray" id="cite-p2-zboray">[3]</a></p><figure class="wiki-figure final-assay-figure final-assay-figure--integrated" data-static-figure-number="11" id="fig-p2-traces"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/a04cbaf62d0e-integrated-vuaa1-traces.svg?v=a04cbaf62d0ef1ab"><picture><source media="(max-width: 650px)" srcset="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/b221621dfc17-integrated-vuaa1-traces-mobile.svg?v=b221621dfc17d6ce"/><img alt="VUAA1 and control time courses in Test 1 and Test 2" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/a04cbaf62d0e-integrated-vuaa1-traces.svg?v=a04cbaf62d0ef1ab"/></picture></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 11. </strong>Normalized VUAA1 and control time courses in Test 1 and Test 2. Test 1 uses each well’s paired baseline; Test 2 uses the first post-addition reading. Each trace represents one well, and panels have independent vertical scales.</figcaption></figure><p>In Test 2, ten of twelve VUAA1 time courses end above their first reading, while eleven conditions have a positive corrected change. Or7a + ORCO and untransfected cells end lower; a positive corrected score does not necessarily mean that the measured trace rises.</p><p class="final-assay-links"><a href="experiments.html#exp-p2-kinetics">Executed method and analysis definitions →</a></p></section><section class="section-block final-assay" data-reveal="" id="kinetic-controls"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">VUAA1 signals and control behavior</h3><p>Figure 12 compares all twelve cell conditions. In Test 2, corrected changes are +36.67 pp for Or35a + ORCO, +18.69 pp for ORCO only and −0.11 pp for untransfected cells.</p><figure class="wiki-figure final-assay-figure final-assay-figure--integrated" data-static-figure-number="12" id="fig-p2-condition-comparison"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/ea49e49aa857-integrated-condition-comparison.svg?v=ea49e49aa8578c4a"><picture><source media="(max-width: 650px)" srcset="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/ef29c85a4d0d-integrated-condition-comparison-mobile.svg?v=ef29c85a4d0d0734"/><img alt="All twelve cell conditions in Test 1 and Test 2" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/ea49e49aa857-integrated-condition-comparison.svg?v=ea49e49aa8578c4a"/></picture></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 12. </strong>All twelve cell conditions. Test 1 shows peak test excursions (%), while Test 2 shows corrected changes (pp); these test-specific summaries are displayed separately.</figcaption></figure><p>Test 1 includes a large positive buffer-control signal, whereas the twelve Test 2 DMSO-control scores range from −1.79 to −0.31 pp. The corrected values are descriptive results from individual wells, rather than activation thresholds.</p><p class="supplementary-data__link"><a href="#results-supplement-odor21">Test 2: all 72 corrected values →</a></p></section><section class="section-block final-assay" data-reveal="" id="kinetic-map"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">Responses relative to buffer and untransfected controls</h3><p>Figure 13 adds the six-stimulus Test 2 screen to the original response maps. Test 2 corrected VUAA1 values range from −0.11 to +39.23 pp. Valeric acid spans −0.77 to +8.17 pp; the other three odor rows range from −2.06 to +1.36 pp. We show vehicle subtraction and the untransfected adjustment separately so that control-dependent changes remain visible.</p><figure class="wiki-figure final-assay-figure final-assay-figure--integrated" data-static-figure-number="13" id="fig-p2-map"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/da8d38c4887a-integrated-response-map.svg?v=da8d38c4887a86a7"><img alt="Corrected response maps from both odor assays" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/da8d38c4887a-integrated-response-map.svg?v=da8d38c4887a86a7"/></a><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/b795ed61cf5d-integrated-control-contrasts.svg?v=b795ed61cf5d6d8d"><img alt="Vehicle and untransfected control contrasts from both assays" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/b795ed61cf5d-integrated-control-contrasts.svg?v=b795ed61cf5d6d8d"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 13. </strong>Response maps with Test 1 on the left and Test 2 on the right, receptors in rows and stimuli in columns, on a shared −60 to +60 pp scale. The first ten receptor labels denote OR + ORCO; hatched Test 1 isopentyl-acetate cells remain excluded, and separate-well control subtraction does not establish receptor specificity.</figcaption></figure><div id="vuaa1-referenced-response"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">Responses relative to each construct’s VUAA1 reference</h3><p>Figure 14 compares each Test 2 odor response with the VUAA1 reference for the same construct, after subtracting its DMSO control. Construct-specific VUAA1 referencing has been used in heterologous receptor assays. <a aria-label="Reference 3" class="cite" href="#p2-ref-zboray" id="cite-vuaa1-reference-zboray">[3]</a></p><figure class="wiki-figure final-assay-figure final-assay-figure--integrated final-assay-figure--reference" data-static-figure-number="14" id="fig-vuaa1-reference"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/0e42619d29e3-test2-vuaa1-referenced-map.svg?v=0e42619d29e373e7"><img alt="Test 2 odor responses normalized to the DMSO-subtracted VUAA1 response of each construct" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/0e42619d29e3-test2-vuaa1-referenced-map.svg?v=0e42619d29e373e7"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 14. </strong>Test 2 VUAA1-referenced responses, with DMSO set to 0 and VUAA1 to 1 for each construct and one well per condition; Or7a–Or98a coexpress ORCO. Orange cells retain negative ratios; †Or7a has a small VUAA1 reference (2.19 pp), making its ratios particularly sensitive to that reference.</figcaption></figure><p class="supplementary-data__link"><a href="#vuaa1-reference-method">Normalization definition and complete ratios →</a></p></div></section>
+</section><section class="section-block final-assay" data-reveal="" id="kinetic-pilot"><span class="anchor-alias" id="odor-screen-20261021"></span><p class="final-assay-kicker">05 · Odor testing</p><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Odor testing</h2><p>We followed fluorescence over time in ten OR + ORCO conditions, ORCO-only cells and untransfected cells. Figures 11–14 show Test 1 and Test 2 time courses, condition comparisons, control-referenced maps and the Test 2 VUAA1-referenced map.</p><p>Test 2 contains 72 wells and 1,440 readings across ten OR + ORCO conditions, ORCO-only cells and untransfected cells. Each construct × stimulus has one well.</p><p>VUAA1 is an established Orco agonist used in heterologous receptor assays. <a aria-label="Reference 2" class="cite" href="#p2-ref-jones" id="cite-p2-jones">[2]</a> <a aria-label="Reference 3" class="cite" href="#p2-ref-zboray" id="cite-p2-zboray">[3]</a></p><figure class="wiki-figure final-assay-figure final-assay-figure--integrated" data-static-figure-number="11" id="fig-p2-traces"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/a04cbaf62d0e-integrated-vuaa1-traces.svg?v=a04cbaf62d0ef1ab"><picture><source media="(max-width: 650px)" srcset="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/b221621dfc17-integrated-vuaa1-traces-mobile.svg?v=b221621dfc17d6ce"/><img alt="VUAA1 and control time courses in Test 1 and Test 2" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/a04cbaf62d0e-integrated-vuaa1-traces.svg?v=a04cbaf62d0ef1ab"/></picture></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 11. </strong>Normalized VUAA1 and control time courses in Test 1 and Test 2. Test 1 uses each well’s paired baseline; Test 2 uses the first post-addition reading. Each trace represents one well, and panels have independent vertical scales.</figcaption></figure><p>In Test 2, ten of twelve VUAA1 time courses end above their first reading, while eleven conditions have a positive corrected change. Or7a + ORCO and untransfected cells end lower; a positive corrected score does not necessarily mean that the measured trace rises.</p></section><section class="section-block final-assay" data-reveal="" id="kinetic-controls"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">VUAA1 signals and control behavior</h3><p>Figure 12 compares all twelve cell conditions. In Test 2, corrected changes are +36.67 pp for Or35a + ORCO, +18.69 pp for ORCO only and −0.11 pp for untransfected cells.</p><figure class="wiki-figure final-assay-figure final-assay-figure--integrated" data-static-figure-number="12" id="fig-p2-condition-comparison"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/ea49e49aa857-integrated-condition-comparison.svg?v=ea49e49aa8578c4a"><picture><source media="(max-width: 650px)" srcset="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/ef29c85a4d0d-integrated-condition-comparison-mobile.svg?v=ef29c85a4d0d0734"/><img alt="All twelve cell conditions in Test 1 and Test 2" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/ea49e49aa857-integrated-condition-comparison.svg?v=ea49e49aa8578c4a"/></picture></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 12. </strong>All twelve cell conditions. Test 1 shows peak test excursions (%), while Test 2 shows corrected changes (pp); these test-specific summaries are displayed separately.</figcaption></figure><p>Test 1 includes a large positive buffer-control signal, whereas the twelve Test 2 DMSO-control scores range from −1.79 to −0.31 pp. The corrected values are descriptive results from individual wells, rather than activation thresholds.</p></section><section class="section-block final-assay" data-reveal="" id="kinetic-map"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">Responses relative to buffer and untransfected controls</h3><p>Figure 13 adds the six-stimulus Test 2 screen to the original response maps. Test 2 corrected VUAA1 values range from −0.11 to +39.23 pp. Valeric acid spans −0.77 to +8.17 pp; the other three odor rows range from −2.06 to +1.36 pp. We show vehicle subtraction and the untransfected adjustment separately so that control-dependent changes remain visible.</p><figure class="wiki-figure final-assay-figure final-assay-figure--integrated" data-static-figure-number="13" id="fig-p2-map"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/da8d38c4887a-integrated-response-map.svg?v=da8d38c4887a86a7"><img alt="Corrected response maps from both odor assays" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/da8d38c4887a-integrated-response-map.svg?v=da8d38c4887a86a7"/></a><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/b795ed61cf5d-integrated-control-contrasts.svg?v=b795ed61cf5d6d8d"><img alt="Vehicle and untransfected control contrasts from both assays" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/b795ed61cf5d-integrated-control-contrasts.svg?v=b795ed61cf5d6d8d"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 13. </strong>Response maps with Test 1 on the left and Test 2 on the right, receptors in rows and stimuli in columns, on a shared −60 to +60 pp scale. The first ten receptor labels denote OR + ORCO; hatched Test 1 isopentyl-acetate cells remain excluded, and separate-well control subtraction does not establish receptor specificity.</figcaption></figure><div id="vuaa1-referenced-response"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">Responses relative to each construct’s VUAA1 reference</h3><p>Figure 14 compares each Test 2 odor response with the VUAA1 reference for the same construct, after subtracting its DMSO control. Construct-specific VUAA1 referencing has been used in heterologous receptor assays. <a aria-label="Reference 3" class="cite" href="#p2-ref-zboray" id="cite-vuaa1-reference-zboray">[3]</a></p><figure class="wiki-figure final-assay-figure final-assay-figure--integrated final-assay-figure--reference" data-static-figure-number="14" id="fig-vuaa1-reference"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/0e42619d29e3-test2-vuaa1-referenced-map.svg?v=0e42619d29e373e7"><img alt="Test 2 odor responses normalized to the DMSO-subtracted VUAA1 response of each construct" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/results/0e42619d29e3-test2-vuaa1-referenced-map.svg?v=0e42619d29e373e7"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 14. </strong>Test 2 VUAA1-referenced responses, with DMSO set to 0 and VUAA1 to 1 for each construct and one well per condition; Or7a–Or98a coexpress ORCO. Orange cells retain negative ratios; †Or7a has a small VUAA1 reference (2.19 pp), making its ratios particularly sensitive to that reference.</figcaption></figure></div></section>
 <section class="section-block" data-reveal="" id="limitations"><span class="anchor-alias" id="discussion"></span><span class="anchor-alias" id="negative-inconclusive"></span><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Discussion</h2><p>The Test 2 VUAA1 time courses show within-acquisition rises in most receptor conditions. After subtracting each well’s F0 change, eleven conditions are positive and the untransfected value is close to zero. Test 1 and Test 2 use different control designs and calculations, so they provide separate observations rather than interchangeable estimates.</p><p>In Test 1, the ORCO-only VUAA1 well showed an 11.86% fluorescence excursion, compared with 0.98% in the untransfected well. The 35.25% excursion in a buffer-only well shows why a rise alone cannot establish ligand-specific activation.</p><p>Colony growth and undigested-plasmid migration document preparation, but neither establishes insert sequence or construct identity. Separate RED and GREEN images likewise do not establish receptor localization or channel function.</p><p>GCaMP6f is a calcium-sensitive indicator. Its resting fluorescence is not a constitutive, calcium-independent measure of transfection. Low green signal may reflect indicator state, expression, acquisition sensitivity or background, and a single endpoint cannot distinguish these explanations. Functional interpretation requires time-resolved, controlled stimulation. <a aria-label="Reference 1" class="cite" href="#micro-ref-1" id="cite-auto-micro-ref-1-1">[1]</a></p><p>The fixed-cell screen at 44–48 h and Test 1 at approximately 72 h are separate pre-selection records. Their position in the workflow does not establish that they came from the same cell batch.</p>
-<p>The microscopy fractions all come from one experiment. Uneven field coverage, within-field clustering and density exclusions prevent ranking receptor conditions as independently replicated estimates of transfection or expression. Blank and Orco-only controls provide within-study context, but do not supply biological replication.</p><p>Test 1 used a laboratory plate reader. These results characterize the biological assay; they do not establish the performance of the assembled AeroSense reader. <a href="#results-supplement-scope">Full assay scope →</a></p><p>Control comparisons use separate single wells and describe fluorescence patterns rather than receptor-specific activation. Test 2 includes a DMSO reference; Test 1 lacks a matched vehicle control. Unrecorded gain, cell-state variation and dilution-only concentration records limit interpretation. Vehicle-matched tests at documented doses in independent cultures are needed to separate these effects.</p><details class="supplementary-data__group" id="results-supplement-scope"><summary>Assay scope</summary><div class="supplementary-data__body"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">Kinetic assay</h3>
-<p>Test 1 contains one well per condition, measured with a laboratory plate reader. Unknown VOC doses, unmatched vehicle conditions, missing numeric gain and buffer-only rises prevent ligand-specific or reproducibility claims. Membrane localization, ionomycin controls and channel currents were not measured. Cell detachment was observed but not quantified; its contribution to the traces is unresolved.</p>
-<h3 class="reading-heading--topic">Endpoint microscopy</h3>
-<p>Positive fractions describe isolated DAPI objects from one batch; dense aggregates were excluded. They do not estimate whole-culture transfection efficiency. <a aria-label="Reference 4" class="cite cite--background" href="#micro-ref-2" id="cite-auto-micro-ref-2-1">[4]</a> GCaMP6f brightness depends on calcium, expression and optical sensitivity, so a green-negative fixed-cell endpoint does not identify an untransfected cell. <a aria-label="Reference 1" class="cite" href="#micro-ref-1" id="cite-auto-micro-ref-1-2">[1]</a> No live/dead stain was acquired; mCherry alone does not establish viability. <a aria-label="Reference 5" class="cite" href="#micro-ref-4" id="cite-auto-micro-ref-4-1">[5]</a></p>
-<h3 class="reading-heading--topic">Scope and provenance</h3>
-<p>No paired measurements connect Test 1 to the assembled AeroSense reader. The CMV constructs have no documented doxycycline induction. All fields, exclusions, thresholds and source hashes accompany the <a href="#data-availability">analysis downloads</a>. <a aria-label="Reference 6" class="cite cite--background" href="#micro-ref-3" id="cite-auto-micro-ref-3-1">[6]</a></p></div></details><p>This map shows response relative to each construct’s reference, not measured transfection efficiency or absolute sensing strength. Each stimulus and reference comes from a separate single well. Or7a’s near-one valeric-acid ratio therefore does not identify it as the strongest responder; the corrected values in Figure 13 preserve the corrected-change magnitudes.</p><p>Stock concentrations were not supplied for Test 2. Repeated time points describe within-well trajectories and do not provide independent biological replication.</p><p>The blank also set the positivity threshold. Its 1/170 fraction is therefore a calibration diagnostic rather than an independently estimated false-positive rate.</p></section>
+<p>The microscopy fractions all come from one experiment. Uneven field coverage, within-field clustering and density exclusions prevent ranking receptor conditions as independently replicated estimates of transfection or expression. Blank and Orco-only controls provide within-study context, but do not supply biological replication.</p><p>Test 1 used a laboratory plate reader. These results characterize the biological assay; they do not establish the performance of the assembled AeroSense reader.</p><p>Control comparisons use separate single wells and describe fluorescence patterns rather than receptor-specific activation. Test 2 includes a DMSO reference; Test 1 lacks a matched vehicle control. Unrecorded gain, cell-state variation and dilution-only concentration records limit interpretation. Vehicle-matched tests at documented doses in independent cultures are needed to separate these effects.</p></section>
 <section class="section-block" data-reveal="" id="conclusion"><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Conclusion</h2><p>The expression screen detected reporter-associated fluorescence above the blank-derived threshold in resolvable DAPI objects. RED-positive fractions across the OR + ORCO conditions ranged from 0.7% to 16.5%; dense overlap prevents interpreting these fractions as whole-culture transfection efficiency.</p><p>The odor assays provide time-resolved evidence of VUAA1-associated fluorescence. In Test 2, F0-subtracted VUAA1 changes are +36.67 pp for Or35a + ORCO, +17.52 pp for Or85b + ORCO, +29.46 pp for Or98a + ORCO, and +18.69 pp for ORCO only, compared with −0.11 pp in untransfected cells. The control maps and retained raw records make these observations inspectable.</p></section>
-<section class="section-block data-availability" data-reveal="" id="data-availability"><span class="anchor-alias" id="methods"></span><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Data availability</h2><p class="publication-status">Code, analysis scripts and reusable documentation are published in the <a href="https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/nthu/aerosense" rel="noopener noreferrer">official iGEM GitLab repository</a>. Archive availability: <a href="https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/nthu/aerosense/-/blob/main/docs/aerosense/archive-status.md" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Research archive availability</a>.</p><details class="supplementary-data__group" id="results-supplement-sources"><summary>Source data and documents</summary><div class="supplementary-data__body"><div class="final-assay-downloads" id="results-odor21-source-files"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">Test 2 odor-assay archive</h3><p>The complete Test 2 raw-data package is prepared locally for Zenodo; no public deposit has been verified. The package contains twelve unchanged XLSX exports, checksums, 1,440 observations with source-cell references, 72 corrected summaries, matched-window sensitivity values and reproducible analysis scripts. The earlier mean-ratio calculation is retained only as a secondary audit metric. All corrected values are available in the supplementary tables.</p></div><div class="final-assay-downloads" id="results-complete-curves-files"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">Complete odor-assay curves</h3><p>The complete-curve analysis preserves the original acquisition boundaries and checks every plotted reading against the canonical exports. The scalable figure, chart-data tables and endpoint audit accompany the single reproducible builder.</p><p><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/downloads/102cb9b79ee9-odor-odor-complete-timecourses.svg">Full-size vector figure · SVG</a> · <a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/downloads/a81f28b9d83f-odor-raw-curves.csv">All 5,280 raw curve readings · CSV</a> · <a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/downloads/6114088f51d3-odor-test2-vuaa1-referenced-curves.csv">Test 2 referenced time courses · CSV</a> · <a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/downloads/f97531b3bf7e-odor-test2-endpoint-audit.csv">Figure 14 endpoint checks · CSV</a> · <a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/downloads/c6c99f96269b-odor-test1-peak-metric-audit.csv">Test 1 peak-metric checks · CSV</a> · <a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/downloads/f536163e8819-odor-source-manifest.json">Source checksums · JSON</a> · <a href="https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/nthu/aerosense/-/blob/main/code/aerosense/wetlab/aerosense-wiki/data/odor-complete-curves-2026-10-21/analysis/build_complete_curves.py">Complete-curve analysis source</a> · <a href="https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/nthu/aerosense/-/blob/main/code/aerosense/wetlab/aerosense-wiki/data/odor-complete-curves-2026-10-21/README.md">Complete-curve reproduction guide</a></p></div><div class="final-assay-downloads" id="results-p2-source-files"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">Test 1 kinetic assay</h3><p><span class="publication-sources">Test 1 source and archive — <a href="https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/nthu/aerosense/-/tree/main/code/aerosense/wetlab/aerosense-wiki/assets/results/final-test" rel="noopener noreferrer">Test 1 analysis source — GitLab</a> · <a class="zenodo-record-link" href="https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/nthu/aerosense/-/blob/main/docs/aerosense/archive-status.md" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Full raw-data archive availability</a></span> — the complete archive preserves 32 exact instrument exports, filename map, source analysis, tidy data, independent calculations, English figures and executed methods. The numerical analysis uses only Python’s standard library. Original inputs are preserved, including the source report whose claims are audited.</p><p><a class="zenodo